To generate a list of command-line arguments for the Java program, we can use the following steps:

1. Define the command-line arguments that need to be passed to the program.
2. Create a string array to store these arguments.
3. Iterate through each argument and append it to the string array.
4. Use the String.join() method to join all the arguments together into a single string.
5. Print the resulting string as the output.

Here's an example implementation using Java:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class CommandLineArgumentGenerator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Define the command-line arguments
        String[] commandArg = {"-d", "-p", "-v", "-o", "-a", "-b", "-c", "-e", "-i", "-j", "-k", "-l", "-m", "-n", "-o", "-p", "-q", "-r", "-s", "-t", "-u", "-v", "-w", "-x", "-y", "-z", "-z"};

        // Create a string array to store the command-line arguments
        String[] commandArgArr = Arrays.toString(commandArg);

        // Iterate through each argument and append it to the string array
        for (int I = 0; I < commandArgArr.length; i++) {
            System.out.println("Command-line argument " + I + ": " + commandArgArr[i]);
        }

        // Join all the arguments together into a single string
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int I = 0; I < commandArgArr.length; i++) {
            sb.append(commandArgArr[i]);
        }

        // Print the resulting string as the output
        System.out.println("Command-line arguments:");
        sb.toString().trim();
    }
}
```

